let's talk about the protests in Lebanon  people have been demonstrating for  months saying they're tired of  corruption and a never-ending economic  crisis for the stop of crushing  liberties freedoms and economic economic  nightmare in which we are in response  Prime Minister Saad Hariri resigned now  Lebanon has a new government but  protesters are still on the streets  they say their message is clear we  haven't given up and we aren't going  away  so why don't they support the new  government and what is the backstory for  what's going on in Lebanon  [Music]  call it an anti-government movement  uprising or even a revolution what's  going on in Lebanon is something many  people there say they haven't seen  before this is a country with 18  officially recognized religious groups  where people have traditionally  supported the interests of their own  faith or sect but this is a new  generation  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  people from across the country took to  the streets the sectarian divide was no  longer there there was no class divide  it was the poor it was the middle class  it was the rich of people who were just  fed up protesters say they're fed up  with a lot of things the economy high  taxes lack of jobs crumbling  infrastructure and they say the biggest  problem is rampant corruption which is  thrived under Lebanon's sectarian  political system we're going to explain  that system in a second  but let's get a sense of just how tough  everyday life is for most Lebanese  people we don't really have access to  proper public services like there's no  24/7 electricity there is no clean  accessible drinking water we have piles  of garbage all over the country sewage  is not treated it goes straight out to  the the ocean so it feels like the state  is absent from daily life in Lebanon  most people agree that mess is caused by  serious mismanagement some say it's  because of corruption but you'll also  hear analysts say that Lebanon never  really recovered from a long and  devastating war  [Music]  we're talking about Lebanon's Civil War  which started in 1975 and went on for 15  years militias from different sects and  political groups fought each other for  dominance and also fought among  themselves then Syria and Israel got  involved and the battles became even  more destructive  at the end of the war Lebanon's main  political groups signed the Taif  agreement to make sure they all got a  slice of political power and that's  really the basis for Lebanon's political  system to date a Maronite Christian gets  the presidency the Prime Minister has to  be a Sunni Muslim and the Speaker of  Parliament is always a Shia Muslim in  Parliament seats are shared 5050 between  Muslims and Christians but in reality  there are two big political alliances  which include parties from different  sects and they each have rival foreign  powers backing them March 14 was former  prime minister Saud Hariri's bloc which  has the support of Saudi Arabia and the  US March 8 is the block that includes  the Maronite Christian president  Michelle Owens party and Hezbollah it  has Syria and Iran support so because of  all of those overlapping and competing  interests many people will tell you that  Lebanon's sectarian political system  isn't really sectarian what's really  happening in Lebanon is that there are  political parties that used to be  militias sects are not fighting in  Lebanon it's really opposing warlords  backed by opposing foreign powers and  this is how many say corruption has  gotten so out of hand  many of those powerful political leaders  or the suami as they're called in  Lebanon are said to have focused on  their own political and financial gains  and made themselves very rich  Lebanon has 220 kilometres of coast 180  kilometers of the coast has been  privatized it's now under the control of  either former officials and people who  are close to them I'll just give you one  example a part meters in Beirut the  revenues we're not going to the state  they were going to a private company  controlled owned by people who are close  to politicians now that's not to say  that everyone in Lebanon wants to  overhaul the entire system the majority  still support the mainly sectarian  political parties for a number of  reasons one ideological - they benefit  from those in power we're talking about  at least 100,000 people employed by the  public sector now with their families  could you imagine that numbers Lebanon's  elite has responded to calls for a clean  up and 2019 Prime Minister Saad Hariri  stepped down and after months of talks  the new prime minister  Hassan Diab who's a computer engineering  professor finally announced his new  government according to diab it's made  up of technocrats with no political  loyalties and it even has a record  number of female ministers but not  everyone is convinced this is real  change  so the protesters had demanded for a  completely independent technocratic  government which just means they're  politically independent and also that  they are experts in their fields it's  kind of just the same thing like  different faces but same parties same  alliances critics of the new government  are saying that many of those  technocrats have the support of the  March 8 Alliance the one that includes  Hezbollah and that complicates things  [Applause]  Hezbollah is a Shia Muslim political  group and arguably Lebanon's most  powerful player it has a military wing  the size of a small army funded by Iran  Hezbollah is considered a terrorist  organization by the US and the EU has  blacklisted its armed wing even just the  perception of Hezbollah pulling the  strings might make it hard for Lebanon's  new government to get the international  funding it desperately needs this is a  country with the world's third highest  level of debt when he compared it to its  small economy its debt as more than 80  billion dollars  Lebanon's former Finance Minister  recently told Reuters news agency that  the country will need at least a twenty  billion dollar bailout possibly from the  IMF World Bank and Gulf countries in  2018 international donors agreed to give  Lebanon 11 billion dollars and loans  grants and donations but Lebanon hasn't  seen that money yet the message that  Beirut is getting is enough is enough  you need to undertake painful but  necessary reforms in order for this  funding to be released people in Lebanon  are already feeling the crisis getting  worse since protests began the value of  Lebanon's currency has fallen by 60% and  banks are already limiting cash  withdrawals to about $200 a week  violence is also escalating as police  try to contain the rage  [Music]  so there's defiance and some hope among  protesters that their campaign will lead  somewhere but other Lebanese people we  spoke to say there's still a very long  road ahead one of our lebanese  colleagues despaired that war-torn syria  might be back on its feet before things  get better in lebanon  start here is the Al Jazeera show that  explains what's going on in the news we  have new episodes coming out every week  so make sure to subscribe like and  follow Al Jazeera's social media pages  so you don't miss our next show I'll see  you next week  [Music]
 

